    handling you can't skip. Not cheap to bolt on if the first version treats notifications as ephemeral UI state.

  [1mMy recommendation: in-app only for the first cycle, but store change events as durable, addressable records from day [0m
  [1mone.[0m You get a shippable slice without standing up mail infrastructure, and the schema that makes email possible later
  is the same schema the in-app feed needs anyway. The trap to avoid isn't "no email" — it's building notifications as
  transient UI state, which is the version you'd have to throw away.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Does a notification need to reach someone who doesn't have the app open? → In-app only, durable events [39m
     [38;5;246m(Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the heart of the request: what "tasks they care about" actually means. This decides the data model, so it's worth
  getting right before anything else.

  The cheap version is implicit — you're notified about tasks assigned to you, derived from a field the task already
  has, no new tables. The expensive version is explicit subscription — a watchers relation, join rows,
  subscribe/unsubscribe UI, and the question of what auto-subscribes you (commenting? being mentioned? creating it?).
  Explicit is strictly more work but it's the only thing that lets someone follow a task they aren't assigned to, which
  is usually the actual request hiding behind "tasks they care about."

  Adding explicit watching later is a migration plus backfill, not a rewrite — so starting implicit is recoverable, just
  not free.
[38;5;246m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[38;5;16m[48;5;153m ☐ Interest [39m[49m

[1m[38;5;231mWhat makes a task one a user "cares about"?[0m

[38;5;153m❯[39m [38;5;246m1.[39m [38;5;153mAssigned or created by them[39m
     [38;5;246mImplicit only, derived from fields the task already has. No watchers table. Smallest data model.[39m
  [38;5;246m2.[39m Implicit + explicit watching (Recommended)
     [38;5;246mAssignee/creator auto-subscribe, plus anyone can watch any task. A watchers relation, and unwatch.[39m
  [38;5;246m3.[39m Explicit watching only
     [38;5;246mNothing is automatic; users opt in per task. Fewest surprise notifications, most friction, easy to miss things.[39m
  [38;5;246m4.[39m Everything in their project/list
     [38;5;246mInterest is scoped by container membership rather than per task. Noisy unless paired with filters.[39m
